PART 1. The examples.
  clock  12, marks [0, 1, 2]: good ways 4 of 8 = 1/2; wound back onto 3: bad share 1/4; 1 - 2*that = 1/2
  clock   2, marks [0, 0, 0, 1]: good ways 4 of 16 = 1/4; wound back onto 1: bad share 3/8; 1 - 2*that = 1/4
  clock  30, marks [0, 1, 2, 3, 9]: good ways 16 of 32 = 1/2; wound back onto 15: bad share 1/4; 1 - 2*that = 1/2
  clock   6, marks [0, 0, 1, 2, 2, 4]: good ways 20 of 64 = 5/16; wound back onto 3: bad share 11/32; 1 - 2*that = 5/16
  clock 210, marks [0, 3, 9, 12, 32, 141, 202]: good ways 72 of 128 = 9/16; wound back onto 105: bad share 7/32; 1 - 2*that = 9/16
  clock   6, marks [0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 2, 2, 4]: good ways 100 of 256 = 25/64; wound back onto 3: bad share 39/128; 1 - 2*that = 25/64
  clock 2, marks 0,0,0,1, all 16 choices by hand rule (sum of signs; sum with the odd hour turned): 4 good of 16
PART 2. The clock of 35 hours, marks 0,1,2,3,4,5,20: falls that fail the severe test at some divisor.
  fail somewhere: 50 of 128 = 25/64 (both ways agree: True); at each divisor: {1: 42, 5: 6, 7: 2, 35: 0}
  for comparison, plain balance somewhere: 8 of 128 = 1/16
  3 in 8 would be 48 of 128
